--TEST--
Every `Core\Http\Client` member answers a `Core\Http\Response`, and the same two readers are written against all five of them — ADR 0074 §§ 5-6, ADR 0058 § 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The depth claim is *invariance over a sweep*, counted rather than read off
// five lines: one shape — `Core\Http\Response $r`, then `$r->status()` and
// `$r->text()` — is written against every member, and the count of refusals is
// what says all five agreed. A member that answered something else, or that
// grew its own reader, takes this off five while every line still reads
// plausibly on its own.
//
// Nothing here reaches a socket: the address is granted, the options are
// bounded, and what stops each call is the transport that is not built yet.
// The readers below the call are therefore compiled and not run, which is the
// half of them a case can assert while that is true.
Core\Http\Target $pinned = Core\Http::allowUrl("http://93.184.216.34/ok");
var $refused = 0;

try {
    Core\Http\Response $a = Core\Http\Client::get($pinned);
    // `status` is a call: a `Core` instance has no property a program can
    // reach, and `a-response-reader-is-a-member-and-never-a-property.nvst`
    // pins the diagnostic the other spelling gets.
    echo "status=", $a->status() as string, " body=", $a->text(), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $one) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}

try {
    Core\Http\Response $b = Core\Http\Client::post($pinned, {retryIdempotencyKey: "k"});
    echo "status=", $b->status() as string, " body=", $b->text(), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $two) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}

try {
    Core\Http\Response $c = Core\Http\Client::put($pinned, {retryIdempotencyKey: "k"});
    echo "status=", $c->status() as string, " body=", $c->text(), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $three) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}

try {
    Core\Http\Response $d = Core\Http\Client::delete($pinned);
    echo "status=", $d->status() as string, " body=", $d->text(), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $four) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}

// A `head` reply has no body at all, and it still answers the same class: the
// readers are the reply's shape rather than a description of what came back,
// so there is no fifth member and no second class for the empty case.
try {
    Core\Http\Response $e = Core\Http\Client::head($pinned);
    echo "status=", $e->status() as string, " body=", $e->text(), "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $five) {
    $refused = $refused + 1;
}

echo "refused=", $refused as string, "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# A literal address is the one host a case can approve without a resolver, for
# the reason `an-approved-url-is-pinned-to-one-address.nvst` gives.
[capabilities.net]
connect = [
  "93.184.216.34",
]
--EXPECT--
refused=5
